*,*::before,*::after{box-sizing:border-box;margin:0;padding:0}:root{--bg:#0d0f17;--sf:#141720;--cd:#1a1e2e;--bd:#252a3d;--ac:#00d4ff;--pp:#7c3aed;--gn:#00ff88;--yl:#ffd700;--rd:#ff4757;--tx:#e2e8f0;--dm:#8892a4;--mn:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;--sn:'Inter',system-ui,sans-serif}body{background:var(--bg);color:var(--tx);font-family:var(--sn);font-size:16px;line-height:1.7}header{background:var(--sf);border-bottom:1px solid var(--bd);padding:2rem 2rem 1.5rem;max-width:1100px;margin:0 auto}.group-badge,.depth-badge{display:inline-block;font-family:var(--mn);font-size:.7rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;padding:.2em .7em;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:.75rem}.group-badge{color:var(--ac);background:#00d4ff1a;border:1px solid #00d4ff4d}.depth-badge{color:var(--pp);background:#7c3aed20;border:1px solid #7c3aed66;margin-left:.4rem}header h1{font-size:clamp(1.6rem,4vw,2.4rem);font-weight:700;line-height:1.2;margin-bottom:.5rem}header h1 span.id{color:var(--dm);font-size:.55em;font-family:var(--mn);margin-right:.5rem;vertical-align:middle}header p.desc{color:var(--dm);font-size:1.05rem;max-width:780px}main{max-width:1100px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 2rem 4rem}section{margin-top:3rem}section>h2{font-size:1.4rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--ac);border-bottom:1px solid var(--bd);padding-bottom:.5rem;margin-bottom:1.5rem}h3{font-size:1.1rem;font-weight:600;color:var(--yl);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem}p{margin-bottom:1rem}ul{padding-left:1.5rem}li{margin-bottom:.4rem}code{font-family:var(--mn);font-size:.85em;background:#00d4ff14;color:var(--ac);padding:.1em .35em;border-radius:3px}pre{font-family:var(--mn);font-size:.75rem;background:var(--cd);border:1px solid var(--bd);border-radius:8px;padding:1rem 1.2rem;overflow-x:auto;color:var(--gn);line-height:1.6;white-space:pre}pre .cm{color:var(--dm)}.viz-card{background:var(--cd);border:1px solid var(--bd);border-radius:12px;padding:1.5rem}.viz-title{font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.3rem}.viz-desc{font-size:.88rem;color:var(--dm);margin-bottom:1.2rem}.preset-row{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.5rem;align-items:center;margin-bottom:.9rem}.rl{font-size:.72rem;color:var(--dm);font-family:var(--mn)}.ctrl-btn{font-family:var(--mn);font-size:.75rem;border:1px solid var(--bd);cursor:pointer;color:var(--dm);padding:.45em .9em;border-radius:6px;background:var(--sf)}.ctrl-btn:hover{border-color:var(--ac);color:var(--ac)}.ctrl-btn.primary{border-color:var(--ac);color:var(--ac);background:#00d4ff14}.sliders{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(210px,1fr));gap:.8rem 1.4rem;margin:.4rem 0 1rem}.sl label{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;font-family:var(--mn);font-size:.7rem;color:var(--dm)}.sl label b{color:var(--ac);font-weight:500}input[type=range]{width:100%;accent-color:#00d4ff;background:transparent}canvas{width:100%;display:block;border-radius:8px;background:#0a0c13;border:1px solid var(--bd);touch-action:none}canvas+canvas{margin-top:.6rem}.stat-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(146px,1fr));gap:.7rem;margin-top:1.2rem}.stat{background:var(--sf);border:1px solid var(--bd);border-radius:8px;padding:.6rem .8rem}.stat .k{font-size:.6rem;letter-spacing:.07em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--dm);font-family:var(--mn)}.stat .v{font-family:var(--mn);font-size:1rem;color:var(--tx)}.stat .v.bad{color:var(--rd)}.stat .v.warn{color:var(--yl)}.stat .v.good{color:var(--gn)}.detail{margin-top:.9rem;border:1px solid var(--bd);border-radius:8px;background:#0a0c13;padding:.85rem 1rem;font-family:var(--mn);font-size:.75rem;line-height:1.7;color:var(--dm)}.detail b{color:var(--tx);font-weight:500}.detail .hl{color:var(--ac)}.detail .er{color:var(--rd)}.legend{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.9rem;font-size:.7rem;color:var(--dm);font-family:var(--mn);margin-top:.8rem}.legend span::before{content:'';display:inline-block;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:2px;margin-right:5px;background:currentColor}.lg-a{color:var(--ac)}.lg-b{color:var(--gn)}.lg-c{color:var(--yl)}.lg-d{color:var(--rd)}#interview-line .quote{background:#7c3aed17;border:1px solid #7c3aed73;border-left:4px solid var(--pp);border-radius:0 10px 10px 0;padding:1.3rem 1.5rem;font-size:1.02rem;line-height:1.75}#interview-line .quote p:last-child{margin-bottom:0}#interview-line .quote em{color:var(--yl);font-style:normal;font-weight:600}#takeaways li strong{color:var(--ac)}.concept-nav{max-width:1100px;margin:0 auto;padding:1.5rem 2rem 3rem;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;border-top:1px solid var(--bd)}.nav-link{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:.2rem;text-decoration:none;padding:.6rem 1rem;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid var(--bd);background:var(--sf);max-width:260px}.nav-link:hover{border-color:var(--ac)}.nav-dir{font-family:var(--mn);font-size:.7rem;color:var(--dm)}.nav-title{font-size:.9rem;color:var(--tx);font-weight:500}@media (max-width:640px){header,main,.concept-nav{padding-left:1rem;padding-right:1rem}.concept-nav{flex-direction:column}.nav-link{max-width:100%}}
.chk{font-family:var(--mn);font-size:.75rem;color:var(--dm);display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.45rem;border:1px solid var(--bd);border-radius:6px;padding:.4em .8em;cursor:pointer}.chk input{accent-color:#00d4ff}.hint{font-family:var(--mn);font-size:.68rem;color:var(--dm);margin-top:.5rem}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<header>
<span class="group-badge">architectures</span><span class="depth-badge">core</span>
<h1><span class="id">13</span>Autoregressive vs Non-Autoregressive TTS</h1>
<p class="desc">
One dependency edge separates the two families, and every difference an interviewer will
probe &mdash; time-to-first-audio, the p99 tail, batching, prosodic variety &mdash; falls out
of it. The total-synthesis-time comparison is a rout and it is also the wrong number.
</p>
</header>

<main>

<section id="theory">
<h2>Theory</h2>

<h3>The only difference that matters</h3>
<p>
Both families turn the same phonemes into the same 80-bin mel and hand it to the same vocoder. What
separates them is one dependency edge. An <strong>autoregressive</strong> decoder computes mel frame
<code>t</code> from the frames before it, so <code>T</code> frames cost <code>T</code> serial forward
passes and <code>T</code> is unknown until a stop token fires. A
<strong>non-autoregressive</strong> decoder predicts a duration per phoneme, expands to a
<code>T</code> known before decoding starts, and computes all <code>T</code> frames in one pass. On the AR side a reduction factor <code>r &gt; 1</code> emits <code>r</code> frames per step and
divides the step count &mdash; the cheapest lever available there, paid for in quality. Everything
below follows from that one edge.
</p>

<h3>Do the arithmetic</h3>
<p>
At 22.05&nbsp;kHz with a 256-sample hop a frame is 11.61&nbsp;ms of audio, so a second of speech is
86 frames. Take an illustrative 1.5&nbsp;ms per AR decoder step on a warm GPU &mdash; the constant is
yours to measure, the structure is not. A five-second reply is 431 frames: <strong>647&nbsp;ms</strong>
of AR mel generation against under <strong>20&nbsp;ms</strong> for one non-autoregressive pass over the
same 431 frames. Vocoding is common to both and cheap: HiFi-GAN V1 was reported at 3.7&nbsp;MHz on a V100, roughly
168&times; real time &mdash; about 30&nbsp;ms for those five seconds, either way.
</p>
<p>
Total synthesis time is a rout, and it is also the wrong number. What the caller hears is
<strong>time-to-first-audio</strong>. The AR decoder can hand its first 30 frames to the vocoder while
still decoding frame 200: 30&times;1.5 + 30&times;0.07 &asymp; <strong>47&nbsp;ms</strong>, and that
figure does not grow with utterance length. NAR cannot do this, because its decoder self-attention is
non-causal over the whole frame sequence &mdash; no frame is final until the pass finishes, so its
TTFA grows with <code>T</code>. On a short conversational turn the single pass is still quicker and
NAR wins both numbers; on a long read-out AR's flat TTFA eventually crosses under it, unless you chunk
the NAR input at clause boundaries &mdash; which production NAR systems do, buying back a flat TTFA at
the price of seams.
</p>

<h3>Where an 800&nbsp;ms turn goes</h3>
<p>
Endpoint hangover ~250&nbsp;ms, ASR finalisation ~80&nbsp;ms, LLM time-to-first-token ~280&nbsp;ms,
egress plus jitter buffer ~90&nbsp;ms: 700&nbsp;ms is gone before the synthesiser emits a sample,
leaving roughly <strong>100&nbsp;ms</strong> for time-to-first-audio. Both families clear that if they
stream, and neither clears it if you wait for a complete utterance &mdash; batched AR on that
five-second reply is 677&nbsp;ms of TTS alone, most of a second budget.
</p>

<h3>The tail is the real argument</h3>
<p>
AR latency is unbounded by construction. Training is teacher-forced and inference is not, so errors
compound, and the stop token is just another prediction. When it never fires the decoder runs to
<code>max_decoder_steps</code>: at 1000 steps, 1.5&nbsp;s of GPU burned emitting 11.6&nbsp;s of babble
the caller may actually hear. It need not be common to matter: at a 0.5%
rate it lands in your p99, and on telephony a destroyed turn is a call the caller ends. Batching makes
it worse &mdash; an AR batch keeps stepping until its longest member terminates, so one runaway item
stalls every call sharing it. NAR batches are uniform: every item does
identical, known work, which is why NAR capacity planning is arithmetic and AR capacity planning is
queueing theory.
</p>

<h3>Quality &mdash; get the direction right</h3>
<p>
The same text supports many valid prosodies. An AR decoder conditions each frame on the ones it just
produced, so a contour it starts it also finishes: it samples one reading rather than averaging them.
A duration or pitch predictor trained with MSE returns the conditional mean instead, which is why
classic NAR output sounds tidy and flat. Do not overstate it &mdash; stochastic duration prediction,
flow and diffusion decoders and adversarial losses have closed much of that gap. The honest sentence
is that non-autoregressive is <em>faster and far more predictable</em>, not more natural, and that
the naturalness gap is narrowing while the tail-latency gap is structural.
</p>

<h3>On a call, and on PHI</h3>
<p>
Neither architecture changes what the data is. The largest latency lever open to both &mdash; caching
audio for phrases you say on every call &mdash; is where the compliance edge sits: cache the fixed
greeting, never the utterance carrying an appointment time and a name, and key on template ID rather
than rendered text, so a caller's details never become a lookup key that outlives the call.
</p>
</section>

<section id="visualization">
<h2>Visualization &mdash; the latency shape of each family</h2>

<pre><span class="cm"># 22050 Hz, 256-sample hop -&gt; 11.61 ms per frame, 86.13 frames per audio second.</span>
T = round(audio_seconds * 86.13)          <span class="cm"># AR: unknown until &lt;stop&gt;. NAR: known up front.</span>

ar_mel  = T * step_ms                     <span class="cm"># T SERIAL passes (step_ms ~ 1.5 ms, illustrative)</span>
nar_mel = base + T*per_frame + T*T*quad   <span class="cm"># ONE pass; quad term = decoder self-attention</span>
voc     = T * 0.07                        <span class="cm"># HiFi-GAN V1: 3.7 MHz on V100, ~168x real time</span>

<span class="cm"># The caller hears FIRST audio, not total synthesis time:</span>
ttfa_ar  = chunk*step_ms + chunk*0.07     <span class="cm"># FLAT in T -- stream mel into the vocoder</span>
ttfa_nar = nar_mel(T)   + chunk*0.07      <span class="cm"># GROWS with T -- nothing is final until the</span>
                                          <span class="cm"># non-causal pass over all T frames ends</span>

<span class="cm"># The tail -- why AR capacity is queueing theory and NAR capacity is arithmetic:</span>
runaway  = max_decoder_steps * step_ms    <span class="cm"># stop token never fires: 1000 * 1.5 = 1500 ms</span>
babble_s = max_decoder_steps * 11.61/1000 <span class="cm"># ... emitting 11.6 s of nothing useful</span></pre>

<div class="viz-card" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
<div class="viz-title">Time-to-first-audio and total synthesis time vs utterance length</div>
<div class="viz-desc">
Log time axis. Solid lines are <strong>first audio</strong>, dashed are <strong>total
synthesis</strong>. AR's first-audio line is flat &mdash; a streamed decoder owes the vocoder only its
first chunk &mdash; while NAR's rises with <code>T</code>, because its decoder self-attention is
non-causal and no frame is final until the pass ends. The red band is the stop-token tail, modelled at
two points: with probability <em>p</em> the decoder never terminates and runs to
<code>max_decoder_steps</code>, so the band spans healthy total to runaway total and p99 takes the
runaway value once <em>p</em> &ge; 1%. Below: the same numbers as a turn, timed from the caller's last
sample.
</div>

<canvas id="cvChart" width="920" height="320"></canvas>
<div class="hint">click the upper chart to move the utterance-length marker</div>

<div class="preset-row" style="margin-top:.9rem">
<button class="ctrl-btn" id="bShort">short phone turn</button>
<button class="ctrl-btn" id="bLong">long read-out</button>
<button class="ctrl-btn" id="bReset">reset</button>
<button class="ctrl-btn primary" id="bPlay">&#9654; play the turn</button>
<label class="chk"><input type="checkbox" id="cStream" checked/> stream AR mel to vocoder</label>
<label class="chk"><input type="checkbox" id="cClause"/> chunk NAR text at clauses (150 frames)</label>
</div>

<div class="sliders">
<div class="sl"><label>utterance length <b><span id="vWrd">12</span> words</b></label>
<input type="range" id="sWrd" min="1" max="60" step="1" value="12"/></div>
<div class="sl"><label>speaking rate <b><span id="vWps">2.5</span> words/s</b></label>
<input type="range" id="sWps" min="15" max="40" step="1" value="25"/></div>
<div class="sl"><label>AR decoder step <b><span id="vStp">1.50</span> ms/frame</b></label>
<input type="range" id="sStp" min="40" max="400" step="10" value="150"/></div>
<div class="sl"><label>NAR pass cost <b><span id="vNar">20</span> ms / 1000 frames</b></label>
<input type="range" id="sNar" min="5" max="80" step="1" value="20"/></div>
<div class="sl"><label>vocoder <b><span id="vVoc">0.07</span> ms/frame</b></label>
<input type="range" id="sVoc" min="2" max="40" step="1" value="7"/></div>
<div class="sl"><label>streaming chunk <b><span id="vChk">30</span> frames</b></label>
<input type="range" id="sChk" min="10" max="120" step="5" value="30"/></div>
<div class="sl"><label>stop-token failure rate <b><span id="vFal">0.5</span> %</b></label>
<input type="range" id="sFal" min="0" max="50" step="1" value="5"/></div>
<div class="sl"><label>max_decoder_steps <b><span id="vMax">1000</span> frames</b></label>
<input type="range" id="sMax" min="300" max="2000" step="50" value="1000"/></div>
<div class="sl"><label>rest of the turn <b><span id="vRst">700</span> ms</b></label>
<input type="range" id="sRst" min="400" max="1100" step="10" value="700"/></div>
</div>

<canvas id="cvTurn" width="920" height="196"></canvas>

<div class="legend">
<span class="lg-a">AR (autoregressive)</span>
<span class="lg-b">NAR (non-autoregressive)</span>
<span class="lg-c">800 ms turn budget / 100 ms TTS slice</span>
<span class="lg-d">stop-token tail &mdash; runs to max_decoder_steps</span>
</div>

<div class="stat-grid">
<div class="stat"><div class="k">mel frames T</div><div class="v" id="stT">&mdash;</div></div>
<div class="stat"><div class="k">audio length</div><div class="v" id="stSec">&mdash;</div></div>
<div class="stat"><div class="k">AR first audio</div><div class="v" id="stFa">&mdash;</div></div>
<div class="stat"><div class="k">NAR first audio</div><div class="v" id="stFn">&mdash;</div></div>
<div class="stat"><div class="k">AR total (RTF)</div><div class="v" id="stTa">&mdash;</div></div>
<div class="stat"><div class="k">NAR total (RTF)</div><div class="v" id="stTn">&mdash;</div></div>
<div class="stat"><div class="k">AR p99 total</div><div class="v" id="stP99">&mdash;</div></div>
<div class="stat"><div class="k">turn to first audio</div><div class="v" id="stTurn">&mdash;</div></div>
</div>

<div class="detail" id="detail">&mdash;</div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="interview-line">
<h2>The line I'd actually say</h2>
<div class="quote">
<p>
"They differ by one dependency edge: autoregressive pays one forward pass per mel frame and doesn't
know how long the utterance is until a stop token fires, non-autoregressive predicts durations,
expands to a known frame count and decodes in one pass. At 22 kHz with a 256 hop that's about
86 frames a second, so a five-second reply is 431 serial steps &mdash; call it 650 ms &mdash; against
under 20 ms for a single pass. But total time isn't what the caller feels; <em>time to first
audio</em> is, and a streamed AR decoder hands the vocoder its first 30 frames in about 50 ms
regardless of length, while NAR's first audio grows with the utterance because its decoder attention
is non-causal."
</p>
<p>
"On a phone call I'd choose on the tail, not the mean. AR latency is unbounded: when the stop token
doesn't fire you burn <em>max_decoder_steps</em>, the caller hears babble, and one runaway item stalls
the whole batch. NAR knows its frame count before it decodes, so capacity is arithmetic. And I'd be
careful with the quality claim &mdash; non-autoregressive is faster and more predictable, not more
natural, and stochastic duration plus flow or diffusion decoders have mostly closed even that gap."
</p>
</div>
</section>

<section id="takeaways">
<h2>Takeaways</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>One edge, every consequence.</strong> Frame <code>t</code> depending on frame <code>t-1</code> is the whole difference: it makes the step count <code>T</code> serial, makes <code>T</code> unknown before decode, and makes latency unbounded. Nothing else about the two families is fundamental.</li>
<li><strong>Compare time-to-first-audio, not total time.</strong> Streamed AR TTFA is flat in utterance length; NAR TTFA grows with <code>T</code>, because its decoder self-attention is non-causal. Clause-chunking the NAR input buys back a flat TTFA and introduces seams &mdash; that is the real production choice.</li>
<li><strong>Faster and more predictable, not more natural.</strong> MSE on durations and pitch returns the conditional mean, which flattens prosody, but stochastic duration prediction and flow or diffusion decoders have closed most of that gap. Get this direction right; interviewers check it.</li>
<li><strong>The tail is the argument on telephony.</strong> A stop token that never fires costs <code>max_decoder_steps</code> &mdash; 1000 steps &times; 1.5 ms is 1.5 s of GPU emitting 11.6 s of babble &mdash; and an AR batch steps until its longest member finishes, so one runaway stalls every call sharing it. Alert on decoder steps per utterance in <strong>Prometheus</strong>, not on mean latency.</li>
<li><strong>Known <code>T</code> is an ops property.</strong> Uniform NAR batches export cleanly to <strong>ONNX</strong> and shape well for <strong>Triton</strong> with a fixed compute budget per item; AR needs a step cap, per-item preemption and queueing headroom instead.</li>
<li><strong>Measure your own constants.</strong> Step, pass and vocoder cost are hardware facts: profile them in <strong>PyTorch</strong> on your serving GPU and re-derive the crossover rather than inheriting this page's numbers.</li>
</ul>
</section>

</main>

